# Daimler Fifteen, 1932-1937.
#
# The 2-litre Fifteen: 2,003 cc six, 63.5 x 105 mm, 45 bhp, fluid flywheel and
# four-speed Wilson preselector, 2,780 mm wheelbase, 1,300 mm track, 25.25 cwt.
# The Fifteen ran from 1932, took the 2-litre engine in August 1934, was
# facelifted for 1936 and enlarged to 2.2 litres in August 1936; the New Fifteen
# succeeded it in 1937. No model total has been found; the Daimler and
# Lanchester Owners' Club calls it the best-selling Daimler of the 1930s.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daimler_Fifteen
# https://www.dloc.org.uk/models
#
# Colours: Black, Dark Green, Maroon and Grey, as specified for this model.
#
# Weight: 10 in total (black 4, green 3, maroon 2, grey 1). Class 3; the Fifteen
# was the cheapest Daimler of its day, so it carries more weight than the
# pre-1920 prestige cars.
#
# Speed 100 km/h: no published maximum speed has been found for the 2-litre
# Fifteen. 100 km/h (62 mph) is an estimate from 45 bhp at 25.25 cwt and is
# flagged for review. Comfort 75/295 (line). Class 3.
# Dates: 1932-1937 with a road tail to about 1962. The model ran from 1932; the two-litre engine replaced the original only in August 1934, and the photographs show the later car.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/daimler-fifteen.blend. References:
# citycars/references/daimler-fifteen/ (gitignored). Images via Wikimedia
# Commons.
#
# Photographs: all four are six-light saloon bodies.
# daimler15-a.jpg 1934 saloon front three-quarter, CC BY 2.0, Sicnag,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1934_Daimler_Fifteen_saloon_(40261155133).jpg
# daimler15-b.jpg 1935 saloon front three-quarter, CC BY 2.0, Steve Glover,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daimler_15_(1935)_(15818775260).jpg
# daimler15-c.jpg 1935 saloon rear three-quarter, CC BY 2.0, Steve Glover,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daimler_15_(1935)_(16005338012).jpg
# daimler15-d.jpg 1934 saloon front three-quarter, CC BY 2.0, sykerabbit77,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daimler_15_HP_1934_4860323967.jpg

